<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kenya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Kenya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kenya, officially the Republic of Kenya, is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, named after Mount Kenya. Its territory holds fossil evidence of early human evolution at Koobi Fora, and was later settled by Cushitic, Nilotic and Bantu speakers. European contact began in 1500 with the Portuguese. The modern country emerged from a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> established in 1895 and the Kenya Colony, which began in 1920. The Mau Mau revolution began in 1952, and Kenya's independence was declared in 1963. After independence Kenya remained a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>, and it is now a presidential representative democratic republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kenya.jpg" alt="Kenya" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pumbaa80 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kenya.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1984, during excavations at Lake Turkana, the palaeoanthropologist Richard Leakey, assisted by Kamoya Kimeu, found the Turkana Boy, a 1.6-million-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a> fossil. Cushitic speakers first settled the lowlands between 3,200 and 1,300 BCE, Nilotic-speaking pastoralists began moving in from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> around 500 BCE, and Bantu people settled the coast and interior between 250 BCE and 500 CE. On the coast, communities of ironworkers, farmers and fishers formed the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, collectively known as Azania. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> built Mombasa into a major port, and the Portuguese voyager <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a> described it as a place of great traffic with a good harbour.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar's coastal possessions in 1885 and handed them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1890 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a>. The Nandi and Kipsigis, led by the Orkoiyot Koitalel Arap Samoei, resisted British colonial establishment by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare and sabotage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-railway">Uganda Railway</a>, and the Nandi were the first group placed in a native reserve. From October 1952 to December 1959 Kenya was in a state of emergency during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-mau-rebellion">Mau Mau rebellion</a>, fought mainly by Kikuyu, Embu and Meru people. Over 11,000 fighters were killed, along with 100 British troops and 2,000 Kenyan loyalist soldiers, and war crimes were committed on both sides, including the Lari and Hola massacres.</p><p>The Republic of Kenya was proclaimed on 12 December 1964 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> as its first president. Daniel arap Moi succeeded him and ran unopposed in 1979, 1983 and 1988; the 1983 election was held a year early after a failed military coup on 2 August 1982. Kenya moved to a multiparty system in 1991 after 26 years of single-party rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwai-kibaki">Mwai Kibaki</a> was elected president, ending Moi's time in office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kenya is named after Mount Kenya. Nairobi is the capital and largest city, and Mombasa the second-largest and oldest. The country covers 580,367 square kilometres and is divided into 47 counties. The 2019 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 47,564,296 people. English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> are the official languages, and most residents are Bantu (60 per cent) or Nilotic (30 per cent).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kenya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1700 the Ajuran state had disappeared, although Ajuran clans survive today in Somalia, Ethiopia and northern <b>Kenya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mengistu wavered between vows of resistance and hints that he would kill himself like Emperor Tewodros II, and on 21 May 1991, saying he was going to inspect troops in the south, slipped into <b>Kenya</b> and flew with his family to Zimbabwe, which granted him asylum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The agreements of 1886 and 1890 between Berlin and London drew the line that became the border of <b>Kenya</b> and Tanzania, surveyed from the sea to Mount Kilimanjaro in 1892 and on to Lake Victoria twelve years later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Kurdish geographer and historian Abu al-Fida (1273–1331), who placed Malindi south of the mouth of a river rising in a mountain hundreds of miles away, perhaps Mount <b>Kenya</b>, where the Galana rises; the town has thus existed as a Swahili settlement since at least the thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mombasa grew up on a coral island separated from the mainland of what is now <b>Kenya</b> by narrow creeks, which gave it one of the best natural harbours on the East African coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The caravan routes it financed carried Islam, the Swahili language, firearms, cloth and new crops deep into the interior, linking the peoples of present-day Tanzania, <b>Kenya</b>, Uganda, Malawi and the eastern Congo to the Indian Ocean economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
